#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:572
msgid "Example: Structured Logging"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:574
msgid ""
"Structured logging allows developers to log data in both a human-readable "
"format *and* a structured format (like JSON) using only a single logging "
"call. This is useful for log aggregation systems that process the structured "
"format while still allowing developers to easily read their logs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:579
msgid ""
"We present two different approaches to implementing structured logging with "
"template strings."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:583
msgid "Approach 1: Custom Log Messages"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:585
msgid ""
"The :ref:`Python Logging Cookbook <python:logging-cookbook>` has a short "
"section on `how to implement structured logging <https://docs.python.org/3/"
"howto/logging-cookbook.html#implementing-structured-logging>`_."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:588
msgid ""
"The logging cookbook suggests creating a new \"message\" class, "
"``StructuredMessage``, that is constructed with a simple text message and a "
"separate dictionary of values:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:603
msgid ""
"The ``StructuredMessage.__str__()`` method formats both the human-readable "
"message *and* the values, combining them into a final string. (See the "
"`logging cookbook <https://docs.python.org/3/howto/logging-cookbook."
"html#implementing-structured-logging>`_ for its full example.)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:608
msgid ""
"We can implement an improved version of ``StructuredMessage`` using template "
"strings:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:643
msgid ""
"Template strings give us a more elegant way to define the custom message "
"class. With template strings it is no longer necessary for developers to "
"make sure that their format string and values dictionary are kept in sync; a "
"single template string literal is all that is needed. The "
"``TemplateMessage`` implementation can automatically extract structured keys "
"and values from the ``Interpolation.expr`` and ``Interpolation.value`` "
"attributes, respectively."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:652
msgid "Approach 2: Custom Formatters"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:654
msgid ""
"Custom messages are a reasonable approach to structured logging but can be a "
"little awkward. To use them, developers must wrap every log message they "
"write in a custom class. This can be easy to forget."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:658
msgid ""
"An alternative approach is to define custom ``logging.Formatter`` classes. "
"This approach is more flexible and allows for more control over the final "
"output. In particular, it's possible to take a single template string and "
"output it in multiple formats (human-readable and JSON) to separate log "
"streams."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:663
msgid ""
"We define two simple formatters, a ``MessageFormatter`` for human-readable "
"output and a ``ValuesFormatter`` for JSON output:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:701
msgid "We can then use these formatters when configuring our logger:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:727
msgid ""
"This approach has a couple advantages over the custom message approach to "
"structured logging:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:730
msgid ""
"Developers can log a t-string directly without wrapping it in a custom class."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:731
msgid ""
"Human-readable and structured output can be sent to separate log streams. "
"This is useful for log aggregation systems that process structured data "
"independently from human-readable data."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:738
msgid "See `logging.py`__ and `test_logging.py`__."
