#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:756
msgid "Backwards Compatibility"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:758
msgid ""
"Like f-strings, use of template strings will be a syntactic backwards "
"incompatibility with previous versions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:763
msgid "Security Implications"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:765
msgid ""
"The security implications of working with template strings, with respect to "
"interpolations, are as follows:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:768
msgid ""
"Scope lookup is the same as f-strings (lexical scope). This model has been "
"shown to work well in practice."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:771
msgid ""
"Code that processes ``Template`` instances can ensure that any "
"interpolations are processed in a safe fashion, including respecting the "
"context in which they appear."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:777
msgid "How To Teach This"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:779
msgid "Template strings have several audiences:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:781
msgid "Developers using template strings and processing functions"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:782
msgid "Authors of template processing code"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:783
msgid "Framework authors who build interesting machinery with template strings"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:785
msgid ""
"We hope that teaching developers will be straightforward. At a glance, "
"template strings look just like f-strings. Their syntax is familiar and the "
"scoping rules remain the same."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:789
msgid ""
"The first thing developers must learn is that template string literals don't "
"evaluate to strings; instead, they evaluate to a new type, ``Template``. "
"This is a simple type intended to be used by template processing code. It's "
"not until developers call a processing function that they get the result "
"they want: typically, a string, although processing code can of course "
"return any arbitrary type."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:796
msgid ""
"Because developers will learn that t-strings are nearly always used in "
"tandem with processing functions, they don't necessarily need to understand "
"the details of the ``Template`` type. As with descriptors and decorators, we "
"expect many more developers will use t-strings than write t-string "
"processing functions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:801
msgid ""
"Over time, a small number of more advanced developers *will* wish to author "
"their own template processing code. Writing processing code often requires "
"thinking in terms of formal grammars. Developers will need to learn how to "
"parse the ``args`` attribute of a ``Template`` instance and how to process "
"interpolations in a context-sensitive fashion. More sophisticated grammars "
"will likely require parsing to intermediate representations like an AST. "
"Great template processing code will handle format specifiers and conversions "
"when appropriate. Writing production-grade template processing code -- for "
"instance, to support HTML templates -- can be a large undertaking."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:811
msgid ""
"We expect that template strings will provide framework authors with a "
"powerful new tool in their toolbox. While the functionality of template "
"strings overlaps with existing tools like template engines, t-strings move "
"that logic into the language itself. Bringing the full power and generality "
"of Python to bear on string processing tasks opens new possibilities for "
"framework authors."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:819
msgid "Common Patterns Seen in Processing Templates"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:822
msgid "Structural Pattern Matching"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:824
msgid ""
"Iterating over the ``Template.args`` with structural pattern matching is the "
"expected best practice for many template function implementations:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:840
msgid ""
"Processing code may also commonly sub-match on attributes of the "
"``Interpolation`` type:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:853
msgid "Memoizing"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:855
msgid ""
"Template functions can efficiently process both static and dynamic parts of "
"templates. The structure of ``Template`` objects allows for effective "
"memoization:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:863
msgid ""
"This separation enables caching of processed static parts, while dynamic "
"parts can be inserted as needed. Authors of template processing code can use "
"the static ``source`` as cache keys, leading to significant performance "
"improvements when similar templates are used repeatedly."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:870
msgid "Parsing to Intermediate Representations"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:872
msgid ""
"Code that processes templates can parse the template string into "
"intermediate representations, like an AST. We expect that many template "
"processing libraries will use this approach."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:876
msgid ""
"For instance, rather than returning a ``str``, our theoretical ``html()`` "
"function (see the `Motivation`_ section) could return an HTML ``Element`` "
"defined in the same package:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:895
msgid ""
"Calling ``str(element)`` would then render the HTML but, in the meantime, "
"the ``Element`` could be manipulated in a variety of ways."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:900
msgid "Context-sensitive Processing of Interpolations"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:902
msgid ""
"Continuing with our hypothetical ``html()`` function, it could be made "
"context-sensitive. Interpolations could be processed differently depending "
"on where they appear in the template."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:906
msgid ""
"For example, our ``html()`` function could support multiple kinds of "
"interpolations:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:918
msgid ""
"Because the ``{attributes}`` interpolation occurs in the context of an HTML "
"tag, and because there is no corresponding attribute name, it is treated as "
"a dictionary of attributes. The ``{attribute_value}`` interpolation is "
"treated as a simple string value and is quoted before inclusion in the final "
"string. The ``{content}`` interpolation is treated as potentially unsafe "
"content and is escaped before inclusion in the final string."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:927
msgid "Nested Template Strings"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:929
msgid ""
"Going a step further with our ``html()`` function, we could support nested "
"template strings. This would allow for more complex HTML structures to be "
"built up from simpler templates:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:941
msgid ""
"Because the ``{content}`` interpolation is an ``Element`` instance, it does "
"not need to be escaped before inclusion in the final string."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:944
msgid ""
"One could imagine a nice simplification: if the ``html()`` function is "
"passed a ``Template`` instance, it could automatically convert it to an "
"``Element`` by recursively calling itself on the nested template."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:948
msgid ""
"We expect that nesting and composition of templates will be a common pattern "
"in template processing code and, where appropriate, used in preference to "
"simple string concatenation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:954
msgid "Approaches to Lazy Evaluation"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:956
msgid ""
"Like f-strings, interpolations in t-string literals are eagerly evaluated. "
"However, there are cases where lazy evaluation may be desirable."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:959
msgid ""
"If a single interpolation is expensive to evaluate, it can be explicitly "
"wrapped in a ``lambda`` in the template string literal:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:969
msgid ""
"This assumes, of course, that template processing code anticipates and "
"handles callable interpolation values. (One could imagine also supporting "
"iterators, awaitables, etc.) This is not a requirement of the PEP, but it is "
"a common pattern in template processing code."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:974
msgid ""
"In general, we hope that the community will develop best practices for lazy "
"evaluation of interpolations in template strings and that, when it makes "
"sense, common libraries will provide support for callable or awaitable "
"values in their template processing code."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:981
msgid "Approaches to Asynchronous Evaluation"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:983
msgid "Closely related to lazy evaluation is asynchronous evaluation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:985
msgid "As with f-strings, the ``await`` keyword is allowed in interpolations:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:998
msgid ""
"More sophisticated template processing code can take advantage of this to "
"perform asynchronous operations in interpolations. For example, a \"smart\" "
"processing function could anticipate that an interpolation is an awaitable "
"and await it before processing the template string:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:1013
msgid ""
"This assumes that the template processing code in ``aformat()`` is "
"asynchronous and is able to ``await`` an interpolation's value."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:1018
msgid "See `aformat.py`__ and `test_aformat.py`__."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:1025
msgid "Approaches to Template Reuse"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:1027
msgid ""
"If developers wish to reuse template strings multiple times with different "
"values, they can write a function to return a ``Template`` instance:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:1038
msgid "This is, of course, no different from how f-strings can be reused."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:1042
msgid "Reference Implementation"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:1044
msgid ""
"At the time of this PEP's announcement, a fully-working implementation is "
"`available <https://github.com/lysnikolaou/cpython/tree/tag-strings-"
"rebased>`_."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:1047
msgid ""
"There is also a public repository of `examples and tests <https://github.com/"
"davepeck/pep750-examples>`_ built around the reference implementation. If "
"you're interested in playing with template strings, this repository is a "
"great place to start."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:1053
msgid "Rejected Ideas"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:1055
msgid ""
"This PEP has been through several significant revisions. In addition, quite "
"a few interesting ideas were considered both in revisions of :pep:`501` and "
"in the `Discourse discussion <https://discuss.python.org/t/pep-750-tag-"
"strings-for-writing-domain-specific-languages/60408/196>`_."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:1058
msgid ""
"We attempt to document the most significant ideas that were considered and "
"rejected."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:1062
msgid "Arbitrary String Literal Prefixes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:1064
msgid ""
"Inspired by `JavaScript tagged template literals <https://developer.mozilla."
"org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/"
"Template_literals#tagged_templates>`_, an earlier version of this PEP "
"allowed for arbitrary \"tag\" prefixes in front of literal strings:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:1072
msgid ""
"The prefix was a special callable called a \"tag function\". Tag functions "
"received the parts of the template string in an argument list. They could "
"then process the string and return an arbitrary value:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:1081
msgid "This approach was rejected for several reasons:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:1083
msgid ""
"It was deemed too complex to build in full generality. JavaScript allows for "
"arbitrary expressions to precede a template string, which is a significant "
"challenge to implement in Python."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:1086
msgid "It precluded future introduction of new string prefixes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:1087
msgid "It seemed to needlessly pollute the namespace."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:1089
msgid ""
"Use of a single ``t`` prefix was chosen as a simpler, more Pythonic approach "
"and more in keeping with template strings' role as a generalization of f-"
"strings."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:1094
msgid "Delayed Evaluation of Interpolations"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:1096
msgid ""
"An early version of this PEP proposed that interpolations should be lazily "
"evaluated. All interpolations were \"wrapped\" in implicit lambdas. Instead "
"of having an eagerly evaluated ``value`` attribute, interpolations had a "
"``getvalue()`` method that would resolve the value of the interpolation:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:1110
msgid "This was rejected for several reasons:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:1112
msgid ""
"The overwhelming majority of use cases for template strings naturally call "
"for immediate evaluation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:1114
msgid ""
"Delayed evaluation would be a significant departure from the behavior of f-"
"strings."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:1116
msgid ""
"Implicit lambda wrapping leads to difficulties with type hints and static "
"analysis."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:1119
msgid ""
"Most importantly, there are viable (if imperfect) alternatives to implicit "
"lambda wrapping when lazy evaluation is desired. See the section on "
"`Approaches to Lazy Evaluation`_, above, for more information."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:1125
msgid "Making ``Template`` and ``Interpolation`` Into Protocols"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:1127
msgid ""
"An early version of this PEP proposed that the ``Template`` and "
"``Interpolation`` types be runtime checkable protocols rather than concrete "
"types."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:1130
msgid "In the end, we felt that using concrete types was more straightforward."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:1134
msgid "An Additional ``Decoded`` Type"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:1136
msgid ""
"An early version of this PEP proposed an additional type, ``Decoded``, to "
"represent the \"static string\" parts of a template string. This type "
"derived from ``str`` and had a single extra ``raw`` attribute that provided "
"the original text of the string. We rejected this in favor of the simpler "
"approach of using plain ``str`` and allowing combination of ``r`` and ``t`` "
"prefixes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:1144
msgid "Other Homes for ``Template`` and ``Interpolation``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:1146
msgid ""
"Previous versions of this PEP proposed that the ``Template`` and "
"``Interpolation`` types be placed in the ``types`` module. This was rejected "
"in favor of creating a new top-level standard library module, "
"``templatelib``. This was done to avoid polluting the ``types`` module with "
"seemingly unrelated types."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:1153
msgid "Enable Full Reconstruction of Original Template Literal"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:1155
msgid ""
"Earlier versions of this PEP attempted to make it possible to fully "
"reconstruct the text of the original template string from a ``Template`` "
"instance. This was rejected as being overly complex."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:1159
msgid ""
"There are several limitations with respect to round-tripping to the original "
"source text:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:1162
msgid ""
"``Interpolation.format_spec`` defaults to ``\"\"`` if not provided. It is "
"therefore impossible to distinguish ``t\"{expr}\"`` from ``t\"{expr:}\"``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:1164
msgid ""
"The debug specifier, ``=``, is treated as a special case. It is therefore "
"not possible to distinguish ``t\"{expr=}\"`` from ``t\"expr={expr}\"``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:1166
msgid ""
"Finally, format specifiers in f-strings allow arbitrary nesting. In this PEP "
"and in the reference implementation, the specifier is eagerly evaluated to "
"set the ``format_spec`` in the ``Interpolation``, thereby losing the "
"original expressions. For example:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:1178
msgid ""
"We do not anticipate that these limitations will be a significant issue in "
"practice. Developers who need to obtain the original template string literal "
"can always use ``inspect.getsource()`` or similar tools."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:1184
msgid "Disallowing String Concatenation"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:1186
msgid ""
"Earlier versions of this PEP proposed that template strings should not "
"support concatenation. This was rejected in favor of allowing concatenation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:1189
msgid ""
"There are reasonable arguments in favor of rejecting one or all forms of "
"concatenation: namely, that it cuts off a class of potential bugs, "
"particularly when one takes the view that template strings will often "
"contain complex grammars for which concatenation doesn't always have the "
"same meaning (or any meaning)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:1194
msgid ""
"Moreover, the earliest versions of this PEP proposed a syntax closer to "
"JavaScript's tagged template literals, where an arbitrary callable could be "
"used as a prefix to a string literal. There was no guarantee that the "
"callable would return a type that supported concatenation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:1199
msgid ""
"In the end, we decided that the surprise to developers of a new string type "
"*not* supporting concatenation was likely to be greater than the theoretical "
"harm caused by supporting it. (Developers concatenate f-strings all the "
"time, after all, and while we are sure there are cases where this introduces "
"bugs, it's not clear that those bugs outweigh the benefits of supporting "
"concatenation.)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:1205
msgid ""
"While concatenation is supported, we expect that code that uses template "
"strings will more commonly build up larger templates through nesting and "
"composition rather than concatenation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:1211
msgid "Arbitrary Conversion Values"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:1213
msgid ""
"Python allows only ``r``, ``s``, or ``a`` as possible conversion type "
"values. Trying to assign a different value results in ``SyntaxError``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:1216
msgid ""
"In theory, template functions could choose to handle other conversion types. "
"But this PEP adheres closely to :pep:`701`. Any changes to allowed values "
"should be in a separate PEP."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:1222
msgid "Removing ``conv`` From ``Interpolation``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:1224
msgid ""
"During the authoring of this PEP, we considered removing the ``conv`` "
"attribute from ``Interpolation`` and specifying that the conversion should "
"be performed eagerly, before ``Interpolation.value`` is set."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:1228
msgid ""
"This was done to simplify the work of writing template processing code. The "
"``conv`` attribute is of limited extensibility (it is typed as "
"``Literal[\"r\", \"s\", \"a\"] | None``). It is not clear that it adds "
"significant value or flexibility to template strings that couldn't better be "
"achieved with custom format specifiers. Unlike with format specifiers, there "
"is no equivalent to Python's :func:`python:format` built-in. (Instead, we "
"include an sample implementation of ``convert()`` in the `Examples`_ "
"section.)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:1236
msgid ""
"Ultimately we decided to keep the ``conv`` attribute in the "
"``Interpolation`` type to maintain compatibility with f-strings and to allow "
"for future extensibility."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:1241
msgid "Alternate Interpolation Symbols"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:1243
msgid ""
"In the early stages of this PEP, we considered allowing alternate symbols "
"for interpolations in template strings. For example, we considered allowing "
"``${name}`` as an alternative to ``{name}`` with the idea that it might be "
"useful for i18n or other purposes. See the `Discourse thread <https://"
"discuss.python.org/t/pep-750-tag-strings-for-writing-domain-specific-"
"languages/60408/122>`_ for more information."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:1250
msgid ""
"This was rejected in favor of keeping t-string syntax as close to f-string "
"syntax as possible."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:1255
msgid "A Lazy Conversion Specifier"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:1257
msgid ""
"We considered adding a new conversion specifier, ``!()``, that would "
"explicitly wrap the interpolation expression in a lambda."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:1260
msgid ""
"This was rejected in favor of the simpler approach of using explicit lambdas "
"when lazy evaluation is desired."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:1265
msgid "Alternate Layouts for ``Template.args``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:1267
msgid ""
"During the development of this PEP, we considered several alternate layouts "
"for the ``args`` attribute of the ``Template`` type. This included:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:1270
msgid ""
"Instead of ``args``, ``Template`` contains a ``strings`` attribute of type "
"``Sequence[str]`` and an ``interpolations`` attribute of type "
"``Sequence[Interpolation]``. There are zero or more interpolations and there "
"is always one more string than there are interpolations. Utility code could "
"build an interleaved sequence of strings and interpolations from these "
"separate attributes. This was rejected as being overly complex."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:1277
msgid ""
"``args`` is typed as a ``Sequence[tuple[str, Interpolation | None]]``. Each "
"static string is paired with is neighboring interpolation. The final string "
"part has no corresponding interpolation. This was rejected as being overly "
"complex."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:1282
msgid ""
"``args`` remains a ``Sequence[str | Interpolation]`` but does not support "
"interleaving. As a result, empty strings are not added to the sequence. It "
"is no longer possible to obtain static strings with ``args[::2]``; instead, "
"instance checks or structural pattern matching must be used to distinguish "
"between strings and interpolations. We believe this approach is easier to "
"explain and, at first glance, more intuitive. However, it was rejected as "
"offering less future opportunty for performance optimization. We also "
"believe that ``args[::2]`` may prove to be a useful shortcut in template "
"processing code."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:1294
msgid "Mechanism to Describe the \"Kind\" of Template"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:1296
msgid ""
"If t-strings prove popular, it may be useful to have a way to describe the "
"\"kind\" of content found in a template string: \"sql\", \"html\", \"css\", "
"etc. This could enable powerful new features in tools such as linters, "
"formatters, type checkers, and IDEs. (Imagine, for example, ``black`` "
"formatting HTML in t-strings, or ``mypy`` checking whether a given attribute "
"is valid for an HTML tag.) While exciting, this PEP does not propose any "
"specific mechanism. It is our hope that, over time, the community will "
"develop conventions for this purpose."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:1306
msgid "Acknowledgements"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0750.rst:1308
msgid ""
"Thanks to Ryan Morshead for contributions during development of the ideas "
"leading to template strings. Special mention also to Dropbox's `pyxl "
"<https://github.com/dropbox/pyxl>`_ for tackling similar ideas years ago. "
"Finally, thanks to Joachim Viide for his pioneering work on the `tagged "
"library <https://github.com/jviide/tagged>`_. Tagged was not just the "
"precursor to template strings, but the place where the whole effort started "
"via a GitHub issue comment!"
msgstr ""

